  "query_preview": "..Widely scattered rain showers with isolated \nembedded thunderstorms will continue overnight, mainly across \nportions of northwest and southeast Utah where two decaying\nmesoscale convective systems are currently tracking through. \nShowers will continue to slowly move northeast across these areas \ninto Wednesday morning.\n\nMonsoon moisture remains in place across the region through\nFriday. A stronger influx of moisture is expected today as the\nridge shifts eastward in response to a deepening trough along the\nwest coast. PWATs are expected to rise to around 1.0-1.1\" areawide\nWednesday as a result, though nocturnal shower activity may \nhinder convective development Wednesday afternoon in some areas if\nconditions remain cloudy and stable. If storms are able to \ndevelop Wednesday afternoon, they will be capable of producing \nwidely scattered showers and thunderstorms that bring an increased\nrisk for flash flooding, especially across southern and central \nUtah. \n\nThe aforementioned trough brings more substantial cooling aloft\nThursday and Friday as it continues to deepen across the West, \nresulting in increased instability across much of Utah and \nsouthwest Wyoming through this period. Increasing upper level flow\nwill also produce more notable amounts of shear that will allow\nfor more organized, widespread convection through this period. \nAs such, the flash flood threat becomes more widespread across\nmost of Utah and southwest Wyoming given deep monsoon moisture\nstill in place. A welcome change arrives in the form of much\ncooler temperatures Thursday and Friday, given the increased \ncloud cover and cooling aloft from this system. Highs will run\nabout 10-15 degrees cooler through this period.\n\nA drier airmass works into the region under southwesterly flow\nthis weekend. Very limited instability will also work to limit\nconvective activity, with storms mainly remaining confined to the\nhigh terrain. The trough that brings drier southwest flow this\nweekend is expected to retrograde westward off the west coast\nearly next week, with our typical monsoonal ridge redeveloping\nonce again in its place.",
